{
  "id": "maximum-width-of-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "maximum-width-of-binary-tree",
  "slug": "maximum-width-of-binary-tree",
  "title": "Maximum Width of Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the relative position doubles every level, same shape as heap indices - but you need it reset per-level, not as a raw global array index, since a deep missing subtree would otherwise inflate the number unrealistically",
    "push (node index, relative position) pairs onto the queue; start with (0, 0) for the root",
    "when processing a level, the width is just (relative position of the last node dequeued this level) - (relative position of the first node dequeued this level) + 1 - you don't need every position, only the two ends",
    "watch for overflow: normalize each level's positions by subtracting the first position's value before computing children's positions, so numbers stay small even in very deep, lopsided trees"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using the node's actual array index (from the 2*i+1 / 2*i+2 encoding) directly as its position, which does NOT reset per level and produces wildly wrong widths for lower levels",
    "computing width from the count of real nodes present on a level instead of from the span between the leftmost and rightmost real node's relative position",
    "letting relative positions grow unboundedly across many levels without renormalizing, causing integer overflow on deep, unbalanced trees"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a\ncomplete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value\n\n**Return the tree's maximum width across all levels**, where a level's width is measured as if the tree were extended to a full binary tree:\n\n- the root gets relative position 0\n- a node at relative position `p` has children at positions `2*p` and `2*p+1`, **reset to start at 0 on each level**\n- a level's width is (rightmost real node's position) minus (leftmost real node's position) plus 1, so any missing nodes between them still count toward that level's width\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, 4, -101, -101, 5]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n   /     \\\n  4       5\n```\n\nOn level 2, node 4 sits at relative position 0 while node 5 - the right\nchild of node 3, whose own left child is the sentinel - sits at relative\nposition 3. The width is 3 - 0 + 1 = 4, even though only 2 real nodes\nexist on that level.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "the answer fits within a 32-bit signed integer"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree, whose only level has width 1",
      "a purely left-leaning or right-leaning chain, where every level's width is 1 despite the tree being tall",
      "a level where the widest gap is created by an entire missing subtree between two distant real nodes"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, -101, -101, 5]",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "level 2 has node 4 (child of node 2, position 0) and node 5 (child of node 3, position 3, since node 3's left child is a sentinel and node 5 is its right child) - width is 3 - 0 + 1 = 4, even though only 2 real nodes exist on that level"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "the last level is completely full with 4 nodes at positions 0, 1, 2, 3 - width 4 - 0 + 1 = 4"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, -101, 3]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "every level here has exactly one real node with nothing missing between it and itself, so every level's width is 1"
      }
    ],
